Getting Started
Prerequisites

Make sure you have the following installed:

Node.js

npm

Git

A Supabase project

1. Clone the Repository
git clone https://github.com/YOUR_USERNAME/YOUR_REPOSITORY.git
cd YOUR_REPOSITORY

Replace the repository URL with your actual GitHub repository.

2. Install Dependencies

Install dependencies for both frontend and backend.

cd frontend
npm install
cd ../backend
npm install
3. Configure Environment Variables

Create a .env file in the backend directory.

Example:

PORT=3000

SUPABASE_URL=your_supabase_project_url
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=your_supabase_service_role_key
SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=your_supabase_anon_key

FRONTEND_URL=http://localhost:5173

Create a .env file in the frontend directory if required by the project.

Example:

VITE_API_URL=http://localhost:3000/api

Never commit .env files, Supabase service-role keys, or other sensitive credentials to GitHub.

4. Set Up Supabase

Create a project in Supabase.

Configure the PostgreSQL database.

Apply the required database schema and migrations.

Configure authentication.

Add the required environment variables.

Ensure the backend can connect to Supabase.

5. Run the Backend
cd backend
npm run dev

The backend will run on the configured port, commonly:

http://localhost:3000
6. Run the Frontend

Open another terminal:

cd frontend
npm run dev

The frontend will run on the Vite development server, commonly:

http://localhost:5173
API Overview

The backend exposes REST API routes for the main application modules.

Typical route groups include:

/api/auth
/api/products
/api/categories
/api/transactions
/api/company
/api/reports
/api/health

The exact endpoints and request formats depend on the implementation.

SKU Generation

SKUs are generated automatically by the backend/database.

Example:

VEER-260912-0002

Where:

VEER = Company prefix

260912 = Creation date in YYMMDD format

0002 = Unique sequential identifier

SKU Rules

SKUs must be globally unique.

SKU generation must not depend on a frontend counter.

Duplicate Part Codes are allowed.

Existing SKUs must remain unchanged.

Concurrent product creation must not produce duplicate SKUs.

The backend/database is responsible for enforcing uniqueness.

Data Integrity

The application is designed to preserve accurate historical records.

Important principles:

Historical transactions retain their original quantities and prices.

Editing a product does not rewrite historical transaction totals.

Stock Movement is based on actual transaction-product relationships.

Product IDs/SKUs are used for internal relationships.

Deleting products must not silently corrupt historical records.

Stock-changing operations are handled safely on the backend.

Duplicate transaction submissions are prevented.

Existing data is preserved during schema changes.

Reports and Export

Reports support:

Date-range filtering

Pagination

Refresh

Print

Excel export

Print and Excel export should use the same filtered dataset displayed by the report.

Excel exports include all matching records, not only the currently visible pagination page.

Development Guidelines

When contributing to the project:

Inspect the existing architecture before making changes.

Preserve working functionality.

Reuse existing components and styles.

Avoid unnecessary dependencies.

Keep frontend and backend logic synchronized.

Do not expose sensitive credentials.

Do not use frontend-only validation for critical business rules.

Test changes on both desktop and mobile.

Do not claim a feature is complete without testing it.

